Wait, actually with SYNC_STAGES=2, we have 2 flops. Data written at cycle 0 (wr_gray updates), then at cycle 1 sync[0] captures it, at cycle 2 sync[1] captures it. So 2 cycles latency. Good.

But there's a subtlety: the memory write happens in wr_clk domain, and the read side needs to see the pointer change before reading the data. Standard async FIFO handles this correctly because the memory write happens at the same edge as the pointer update, and the pointer sync delay ensures the data is stable before the read side sees the pointer.

Now, reset handling: R2 says de-assertion is per-domain. So we need to handle the case where one side comes out first. The standard async FIFO handles this because:
- If write side comes out first, it can write data but read side won't see it until read side comes out and syncs.
- If read side comes out first, it sees empty (wr_gray_sync = 0) so no phantom reads.

