```systemverilog
module async_fifo_cdc #(
    parameter int DATA_W      = 32,
    parameter int LOG_DEPTH   = 3,
    parameter int SYNC_STAGES = 2
) (
    input  logic              wr_clk,
    input  logic              wr_rst_n,
    input  logic              wr_valid,
    output logic              wr_ready,
    input  logic [DATA_W-1:0] wr_data,

    input  logic              rd_clk,
    input  logic              rd_rst_n,
    output logic              rd_valid,
    input  logic              rd_ready,
    output logic [DATA_W-1:0] rd_data
);

    localparam int DEPTH = 1 << LOG_DEPTH;
    localparam int PTR_W = LOG_DEPTH + 1;

    // Memory
    logic [DATA_W-1:0] mem [DEPTH];

    // Write domain
    logic [PTR_W-1:0] wr_bin, wr_bin_next;
    logic [PTR_W-1:0] wr_gray, wr_gray_next;
    logic             wr_full;
    logic [PTR_W-1:0] rd_gray_sync [SYNC_STAGES];
    logic [PTR_W-1:0] rd_bin_sync;

    // Read domain
    logic [PTR_W-1:0] rd_bin, rd_bin_next;
    logic [PTR_W-1:0] rd_gray, rd_gray_next;
    logic             rd_empty;
    logic [PTR_W-1:0] wr_gray_sync [SYNC_STAGES];
    logic [PTR_W-1:0] wr_bin_sync;

    // Binary to Gray
    function automatic logic [PTR_W-1:0] bin2gray(input logic [PTR_W-1:0] b);
        return b ^ (b >> 1);
    endfunction

    // Gray to Binary
    function automatic logic [PTR_W-1:0] gray2bin(input logic [PTR_W-1:0] g);
        logic [PTR_W-1:0] b;
        b[PTR_W-1] = g[PTR_W-1];
        for (int i = PTR_W-2; i >= 0; i--) b[i] = b[i+1] ^ g[i];
        return b;
    endfunction

    // ---- Write side ----
    always_comb begin
        wr_bin_next  = wr_bin + 1'b1;
        wr_gray_next = bin2gray(wr_bin_next);
    end

    // Sync rd_gray into wr domain
    always_ff @(posedge wr_clk or negedge wr_rst_n) begin
        if (!wr_rst_n) begin
            for (int i = 0; i < SYNC_STAGES; i++) rd_gray_sync[i] <= '0;
        end else begin
            rd_gray_sync[0] <= rd_gray;
            for (int i = 1; i < SYNC_STAGES; i++) rd_gray_sync[i] <= rd_gray_sync[i-1];
        end
    end

    assign rd_bin_sync = gray2bin(rd_gray_sync[SYNC_STAGES-1]);

    // Full detection: full when wr_bin_next == rd_bin_sync + DEPTH
    // Equivalently: MSB differs and lower bits equal
    assign wr_full = (wr_bin_next[PTR_W-1] != rd_bin_sync[PTR_W-1]) &&
                     (wr_bin_next[PTR_W-2:0] == rd_bin_sync[PTR_W-2:0]);

    assign wr_ready = !wr_full;

    // Write
    always_ff @(posedge wr_clk or negedge wr_rst_n) begin
        if (!wr_rst_n) begin
            wr_bin  <= '0;
            wr_gray <= '0;
        end else if (wr_valid && wr_ready) begin
            mem[wr_bin[LOG_DEPTH-1:0]] <= wr_data;
            wr_bin  <= wr_bin_next;
            wr_gray <= wr_gray_next;
        end
    end

    // ---- Read side ----
    always_comb begin
        rd_bin_next  = rd_bin + 1'b1;
        rd_gray_next = bin2gray(rd_bin_next);
    end

    // Sync wr_gray into rd domain
    always_ff @(posedge rd_clk or negedge rd_rst_n) begin
        if (!rd_rst_n) begin
            for (int i = 0; i < SYNC_STAGES; i++) wr_gray_sync[i] <= '0;
        end else begin
            wr_gray_sync[0] <= wr_gray;
            for (int i = 1; i < SYNC_STAGES; i++) wr_gray_sync[i] <= wr_gray_sync[i-1];
        end
    end

    assign wr_bin_sync = gray2bin(wr_gray_sync[SYNC_STAGES-1]);

    // Empty detection
    assign rd_empty = (rd_bin == wr_bin_sync);

    assign rd_valid = !rd_empty;
    assign rd_data  = mem[rd_bin[LOG_DEPTH-1:0]];

    // Read
    always_ff @(posedge rd_clk or negedge rd_rst_n) begin
        if (!rd_rst_n) begin
            rd_bin  <= '0;
            rd_gray <= '0;
        end else if (rd_valid && rd_ready) begin
            rd_bin  <= rd_bin_next;
            rd_gray <= rd_gray_next;
        end
    end

endmodule
```

Wait, there's an issue with `wr_ready` and H1. H1 says wr_ready must not depend combinationally on wr_valid. In my code, wr_ready = !wr_full, and wr_full depends on wr_bin_next and rd_bin_sync. wr_bin_next = wr_bin + 1. wr_bin is a register. So wr_ready doesn't depend on wr_valid. Good.

But wait, wr_bin_next is computed combinationally from wr_bin, and wr_full depends on wr_bin_next. So wr_ready depends on wr_bin (register) and rd_bin_sync (register output). Not on wr_valid. Good.

Similarly rd_valid = !rd_empty, rd_empty depends on rd_bin (register) and wr_bin_sync (register output). Not on rd_ready. Good.

Now let me think about the SYNC_STAGES latency requirement more carefully. The spec says: "a beat cannot reach the read side until the write pointer has crossed SYNC_STAGES flops clocked by rd_clk, so the MINIMUM observed crossing latency must be at least SYNC_STAGES rd_clk cycles."

With my code, when wr side writes, wr_gray updates at the next wr_clk edge. Then rd side: wr_gray_sync[0] captures wr_gray at next rd_clk edge. Then wr_gray_sync[1] captures wr_gray_sync[0] at next rd_clk edge. So with SYNC_STAGES=2, minimum latency is 2 rd_clk cycles. Good.

But wait, there's a subtlety: the wr_gray update happens at the wr_clk edge, but the actual data write to mem also happens at that edge. So the data is available in mem at that edge. Then rd side needs to see the pointer change. With 2 stages, it takes 2 rd_clk cycles minimum. During that time, the data is stable in mem. Good.

Hmm, but there's a potential issue: what if the wr_clk edge and rd_clk edge are very close? Then the sync might capture a value that's changing. That's why we use Gray code - only one bit changes at a time, so the sync either captures the old or new value, both valid.

But wait, with Gray code, if multiple bits were changing, we could capture a mix. With Gray code, only one bit changes per increment, so the sync captures either the old or new value. But what if the wr_gray changes multiple times between rd_clk edges? Then the sync captures the latest value (which is a valid Gray code). The intermediate values are skipped, but that's fine - we just don't see those pointers.

Actually wait, there's a subtle issue with Gray code and multiple increments between samples. If wr_gray goes from A to B to C (each one bit change), and rd side samples during the transition from B to C, it might capture B (old) or C (new), both valid. Good.

But if wr_gray goes from A to B to C to D, and rd side samples during A->B transition, it captures A or B. Then next sample captures D. So it skips B, C. But the pointer just needs to be monotonic - it doesn't need to see every value. As long as the final value is correct and we don't miss data, it's fine.

Actually, wait. If rd side skips from A to D, that means rd side thinks wr pointer is at D. But rd side needs to read all data from A to D. Since rd side reads sequentially from its own pointer, it will read all entries. The wr pointer just tells it how far it can go. So skipping intermediate pointer values is fine.

OK, I think the design is correct. Let me double-check the full detection.

Standard formula: full when wr_bin_next[MSB] != rd_bin_sync[MSB] && wr_bin_next[MSB-1:0] == rd_bin_sync[MSB-1:0].

Let's verify: with DEPTH=8, PTR_W=4. Suppose rd_bin_sync = 0 (0000). Then full when wr_bin_next = 1000 (8). So wr_bin can go from 0 to 7 (7 entries written), and at wr_bin=7, wr_bin_next=8, which is full. So wr_ready goes low when wr_bin=7. That means 8 entries can be written (0-7). Good, that's DEPTH=8.

Wait, let me recount. Initially wr_bin=0, rd_bin_sync=0. wr_bin_next=1, not full. Write at address 0, wr_bin becomes 1. Continue: wr_bin=7, wr_bin_next=8. Is 8 full? 8[MSB]=1, 0[MSB]=0, so MSB differs. Lower bits: 8[2:0]=000, 0[2:0]=000, equal. So full. So wr_ready is low when wr_bin=7. That means we can write when wr_bin=0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7 - that's 8 writes. Good, DEPTH=8.

Now let me think about the reset. R4 says wr_ready becomes 1 within 16 wr_clk cycles of wr_rst_n releasing. In my code, wr_rst_n releases, wr_bin=0, rd_bin_sync=0 (since rd_gray_sync was reset to 0). wr_bin_next=1, not full. So wr_ready=1 immediately. Good.

R5 says rd_valid=0 while rd_rst_n is low. In my code, rd_valid = !rd_empty, rd_empty = (rd_bin == wr_bin_sync). During reset, rd_bin=0, wr_bin_sync=0 (reset). So rd_empty=1, rd_valid=0. Good.

But wait, what if rd_rst_n releases before wr_rst_n? Then rd_bin=0, wr_bin_sync=0 (still reset). rd_empty=1, rd_valid=0. Good.

What if wr_rst_n releases first, and wr side starts writing? wr_bin advances, wr_gray advances. rd side is still in reset, so wr_gray_sync is held at 0. When rd_rst_n releases, wr_gray_sync starts syncing. It will eventually see the current wr_gray. rd side will read from rd_bin=0. Good.

But there's a subtle issue: what if wr side writes more than DEPTH entries while rd side is in reset? Then wr_ready goes low (full). So wr side stops. No overflow. Good.

Now, one more concern: the memory. When wr side writes mem[wr_bin[LOG_DEPTH-1:0]] <= wr_data, and rd side reads mem[rd_bin[LOG_DEPTH-1:0]]. These are different clock domains accessing the same memory. In simulation, this works because the memory is just an array. In synthesis, this is a dual-port RAM (one write port, one read port). That's standard for async FIFO.

